WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.304
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:08.572
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:08.872 --> 00:00:15.553
Unit one: Language and Thought. In this unit, you will explore how language shapes the way we think.

4
00:00:16.550 --> 00:00:18.480
linguistic relativity.

5
00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:25.208
linguistic relativity means the idea that the language you speak influences how you think.

6
00:00:25.508 --> 00:00:33.510
Linguistic relativity suggests that Khmer speakers may notice relationships differently from English speakers.

7
00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:37.756
Your turn. Say: linguistic relativity.

8
00:00:40.456 --> 00:00:42.235
linguistic relativity.

9
00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:44.251
nuance.

10
00:00:44.951 --> 00:00:49.470
nuance means a very small difference in meaning, feeling or tone.

11
00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.861
Translation apps often miss the nuance of a joke.

12
00:00:53.561 --> 00:00:56.306
Your turn. Say: nuance.

13
00:00:59.006 --> 00:00:59.914
nuance.

14
00:01:00.917 --> 00:01:02.115
connotation.

15
00:01:02.815 --> 00:01:07.996
connotation means the feeling or idea a word suggests beyond its basic meaning.

16
00:01:08.296 --> 00:01:12.571
'Cheap' has a negative connotation, but 'affordable' sounds positive.

17
00:01:13.271 --> 00:01:16.225
Your turn. Say: connotation.

18
00:01:18.925 --> 00:01:20.100
connotation.

19
00:01:21.117 --> 00:01:22.362
ambiguity.

20
00:01:23.062 --> 00:01:27.836
ambiguity means the possibility of understanding something in more than one way.

21
00:01:28.136 --> 00:01:31.354
The ambiguity in the contract caused a long dispute.

22
00:01:32.054 --> 00:01:35.044
Your turn. Say: ambiguity.

23
00:01:37.744 --> 00:01:39.000
ambiguity.

24
00:01:40.017 --> 00:01:41.366
articulate thought.

25
00:01:42.066 --> 00:01:46.724
articulate thought means an idea expressed clearly and precisely.

26
00:01:47.024 --> 00:01:51.833
Writing every day helps turn feelings into articulate thought.

27
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:55.673
Your turn. Say: articulate thought.

28
00:01:58.373 --> 00:01:59.792
articulate thought.

29
00:02:00.783 --> 00:02:01.819
inner voice.

30
00:02:02.519 --> 00:02:05.947
inner voice means the voice you 'hear' in your head when you think.

31
00:02:06.247 --> 00:02:09.535
My inner voice now speaks English when I study.

32
00:02:10.235 --> 00:02:13.282
Your turn. Say: inner voice.

33
00:02:15.982 --> 00:02:17.134
inner voice.

34
00:02:18.150 --> 00:02:19.360
semantics.

35
00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.849
semantics means the study of meaning in language.

36
00:02:23.149 --> 00:02:26.553
The argument was really about semantics, not facts.

37
00:02:27.253 --> 00:02:29.964
Your turn. Say: semantics.

38
00:02:32.664 --> 00:02:33.677
semantics.

39
00:02:34.683 --> 00:02:36.114
bilingual advantage.

40
00:02:36.814 --> 00:02:42.622
bilingual advantage means the mental benefits some researchers link to speaking two languages.

41
00:02:42.922 --> 00:02:46.744
The bilingual advantage may include better focus and flexibility.

42
00:02:47.444 --> 00:02:50.863
Your turn. Say: bilingual advantage.

43
00:02:53.563 --> 00:02:55.109
bilingual advantage.

44
00:02:56.117 --> 00:02:58.337
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:02:58.637 --> 00:03:02.448
Rithy, do you ever think in English rather than in Khmer?

46
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:09.328
Increasingly, yes. I'd been studying for years before my inner voice started switching languages.

47
00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:17.669
That's fascinating. Some researchers argue for linguistic relativity: language shapes how we see the world.

48
00:03:18.983 --> 00:03:23.934
I'm not entirely convinced. Surely thought comes first and language simply labels it?

49
00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:32.825
Perhaps, but consider connotation. 'Cheap' and 'affordable' describe the same price, yet they make us feel quite differently.

50
00:03:34.117 --> 00:03:39.869
True. And Khmer has words for family relationships that English simply doesn't distinguish.

51
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:46.076
Exactly. Those nuances may train speakers to notice different things.

52
00:03:47.383 --> 00:03:52.439
So being bilingual gives me two lenses rather than one. I like that idea.

53
00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:57.654
Grammar focus: Narrative tenses for nuance.

54
00:03:57.954 --> 00:04:02.577
At C1 level, narrative tenses express nuance as well as time.

55
00:04:02.877 --> 00:04:13.120
Use the past perfect continuous to stress duration, the past continuous for interrupted actions, and the past perfect for completed earlier events.

56
00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:18.136
For example: I had been studying for years before I started thinking in English.

57
00:04:18.436 --> 00:04:24.870
At C1, choose tenses not just for time but for emphasis: duration, interruption or completion.

58
00:04:25.883 --> 00:04:28.965
Now read and listen. Do We Think in Words?.

59
00:04:29.265 --> 00:04:33.935
For centuries, philosophers have argued about whether thought is possible without language.

60
00:04:35.250 --> 00:04:41.220
The theory of linguistic relativity claims that the language we speak shapes how we perceive reality.

61
00:04:42.517 --> 00:04:48.324
Early versions of the theory were extreme, suggesting that language completely determines thought.

62
00:04:49.617 --> 00:04:55.935
Modern research supports a weaker version: language influences, but does not control, our thinking.

63
00:04:57.250 --> 00:05:03.278
For example, speakers of languages with many words for colours may distinguish shades more quickly.

64
00:05:04.583 --> 00:05:10.693
Bilingual people often report that their personality feels slightly different in each language.

65
00:05:11.983 --> 00:05:17.686
This may be because each language carries its own connotations, values and cultural memories.

66
00:05:18.983 --> 00:05:23.665
Language, then, is not a prison for the mind, but it is certainly a lens.

67
00:05:24.950 --> 00:05:27.684
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:27.984 --> 00:05:32.247
The feeling a word suggests beyond its basic meaning is its blank.

69
00:05:32.547 --> 00:05:36.572
A, semantics. B, connotation. C, ambiguity.

70
00:05:40.583 --> 00:05:43.268
The answer is B: connotation.

71
00:05:44.783 --> 00:05:47.839
I blank for an hour when I finally understood the text.

72
00:05:48.139 --> 00:05:53.070
A, was reading. B, had been reading. C, have read.

73
00:05:57.083 --> 00:06:00.058
The answer is B: had been reading.

74
00:06:01.550 --> 00:06:04.896
While she blank the report, she noticed an error.

75
00:06:05.196 --> 00:06:09.035
A, was checking. B, had checked. C, checks.

76
00:06:13.050 --> 00:06:15.758
The answer is A: was checking.

77
00:06:17.250 --> 00:06:21.432
The possibility of understanding something in two ways is blank.

78
00:06:21.732 --> 00:06:26.245
A, nuance. B, ambiguity. C, inner voice.

79
00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.656
The answer is B: ambiguity.

80
00:06:34.150 --> 00:06:38.785
According to the text, modern research supports which version of the theory?

81
00:06:39.085 --> 00:06:45.478
A, an extreme version. B, a weaker version. C, no version.

82
00:06:49.483 --> 00:06:52.238
The answer is B: a weaker version.

83
00:06:53.750 --> 00:06:59.061
Now it's your turn. Part one: Does the language you speak change the way you think?

84
00:07:04.361 --> 00:07:09.460
Part two: Give two reasons and an example from Khmer and English.

85
00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:29.770
I believe it does, to some extent. Firstly, Khmer forces me to notice age and status when I speak to people; secondly, English lets me be more direct. For example, I find it easier to disagree politely in English.

86
00:07:30.070 --> 00:07:32.255
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:38.027
Great work today! You learned eight new words and narrative tenses for nuance.

88
00:07:38.327 --> 00:07:42.523
Today's value is curiosity. Ask questions and find out.

89
00:07:42.823 --> 00:07:45.220
See you in Unit two. Goodbye!

90
00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:48.033
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

